body:
Traditional information systems were largely organized around centralized databases and a relatively small number of application servers. This model remains effective when data volume is moderate, operational contexts are stable, and the number of consumers remains limited. However, once organizations begin collecting heterogeneous data from multiple channels, centralized architectures become difficult to sustain. Storage pressure increases, computational workloads become more varied, and the system becomes more sensitive to single-node bottlenecks.

The centralized model is simple to deploy, but it concentrates ingestion, storage, processing, metadata, and access on the same execution point. As workloads grow, this concentration creates bottlenecks, single points of failure, and clear scalability limits.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \input{90_assets/latex_diagrams/chapter_2/fig_2_1_centralized_architecture.tex}
  \caption{Centralized architecture as a concentration of ingestion, storage, processing, and access on one execution point.}

\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:centralized-architecture} highlights why centralized designs become fragile under heterogeneous analytical growth: all responsibilities compete on the same machine boundary, and operational evolution is constrained by the limits of that boundary.

Distributed data platforms emerged as a response to these limits. Their role is not only to increase capacity, but also to separate responsibilities such as ingestion, storage, compute, metadata, and analytical access. This decomposition allows each concern to evolve with the technical model best suited to it \cite{kleppmann2017ddia}.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \input{90_assets/latex_diagrams/chapter_2/fig_2_2_distributed_data_platform.tex}
  \caption{Distributed data platform as a layered decomposition of storage, compute, metadata, and analytical access responsibilities.}

\end{figure}

By contrast, the distributed platform separates responsibilities according to operational role. This supports modular growth, parallel execution, better resilience, and clearer alignment between storage and processing patterns. Figure~\ref{fig:distributed-data-platform} therefore prepares the discussion on horizontal scaling, reliability, and maintainability.

