Figure~\ref{fig:spark-execution-model} illustrates the operational logic behind this principle: a driver coordinates execution, a cluster manager allocates resources, executors process partitions in parallel, and the SQL engine materializes the physical plan that structures distributed analytical work.

The historical movement from warehouses to lakes, from lakes to lakehouses, and from isolated reporting toward event-aware architectures reflects a broader shift in priorities. Early architectures privileged control and stable relational semantics. Later architectures privileged ingestion flexibility and scale. Contemporary platforms increasingly seek a hybrid answer that preserves raw fidelity, enables distributed processing, and still produces governed analytical products.

For \projectname, the lakehouse model is the most suitable. It supports raw bronze persistence, distributed storage, structured analytical tables, metadata-driven reuse, and downstream SQL exploration. The selected architecture is therefore a reasoned synthesis of the state of the art.

More precisely, the implemented project combines both Lambda-style and Kappa-style reasoning without reproducing either model in a pure textbook form. It remains Kappa-inspired because replayable incoming records and durable raw history are treated as operational assets rather than disposable transit. At the same time, it remains close to Lambda-style discipline because the main analytical semantics are stabilized through batch-oriented refinement over preserved historical data. The resulting architecture is therefore best understood as a governed hybrid lakehouse model: event-aware at ingestion time, but structurally batch-oriented when producing trusted analytical layers \cite{marz2015bigdata,kreps2014questioning,armbrust2021lakehouse}.

